<apex:page standardController="Opportunity" 
           extensions="Pages"
           renderAs="pdf" 
           showHeader="false"
           sidebar="false" 
           applyBodyTag="false"
           applyHtmlTag="false"> 
      <html> 
        <head> 
           <style>
             .heading{
                font-size: 35px;
                font-weight: bold; 
                text-align: center; 
                color:rgb(3,45,96);
             } 
             .main{
                  font-size: 25px;
                  text-align: center;
             } 
             .productTable{
               width: 100%;
               border-collapse:collapse;
               margin-top: 15px;
             }
             .productTable th{
                border: 1px solid rgb(5, 3, 25);
                padding: 8px; 
                color:white
                text-align: center; 
                background-color:rgb(3, 45, 96);
             } 
             .productTable td{
                  border: 1px solid black;
                  padding: 8px;
                  text-align: left;
             }
             p{
                  font-size: 8px; 
                  text-align: left;
             }
             .firstPara{
                margin-bottom: 8px;
                font-size:20px;

   
             }
             .secondPara{
                  margin-top: 0; 
                  font-size:20px;
             }  
             h1{
               color:rgb(3,45,96);
             }
             h2{
                  color:rgb(3,45,96);
             } 
             h3{ 
               text-align:left;
               font-size:25px;
               color:rgb(10, 122, 213);

             }
             .product{
               color:rgb(3, 45, 96);
             }
             li{
               font-size:20px;
             } 
             li::marker{
               color:rgb(10,122,213)
             } 
            </style>
        </head> 
        <body>
         <div
            class="heading" title="Salesforce">
               <b>Salesforce Platfrom events</b>
         </div> 
         <p class="main">Training Guide&amp;Hands-On PraciceTasks(Begineer to Adavanced)</p> 
         <hr/>
         <h1>What Are Platform Events?</h1>
          <p class="firstPara">Platform Events are Salesforce's implementation of an event-driven architecture — a lightweight, secure way to publish and subscribe to real-time data changes, both within Salesforce and with external systems, without polling or tight coupling.</p>
          <p class="secondPara">Think of it like a notification bus: something happens ("Order Shipped"), an event is published, and any number of subscribers (Flows, Apex triggers, external apps, Lightning components) react independently — the publisher doesn't need to know who is listening</p> 
          <h2>Key Building Blocks</h2> 
          <table class="productTable"> 
            <tr>
               <th>Product Name</th>
               <th>Quantity</th>
               <th>Unit Price</th>
               <th>Total Price</th>
            </tr>
            <apex:repeat value="{!opps}" var="lineItem">
            <tr>
               <td>{!lineItem.PricebookEntry.Product2.Name}</td>
               <td>{!lineItem.Quantity}</td>
               <td>{!lineItem.UnitPrice}</td>
               <td>{!lineItem.TotalPrice}</td>
            </tr>
            </apex:repeat> 
          </table>
        <h3>Exact Use Cases</h3> 
           <ul>
              <li>
                 <b>Decoupling systems</b> — e.g., an Order Management system publishes "Order Created" and Shipping,
                 Billing, and Notifications all subscribe independently, with no direct dependency between them.
              </li>
              <li>
                    <b>Integration with external systems</b> — publish events that middleware (MuleSoft, external apps via
                    CometD) consumes in near real time, instead of external systems polling Salesforce APIs  
              </li>
                <li>
                  <b> Async processing to avoid governor limits</b> — instead of doing everything synchronously in one trigger,
                   publish an event and let a separate subscriber handle heavy logic 
                </li> 
            </ul>  
         </body> 
      </html> 
</apex:page>